<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-logic-arguments" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Arguments</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Understand the structure of logical arguments by identifying the premise(s) and conclusion.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Translate between symbolic logic and natural language.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Distinguish between deductive and inductive arguments.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Determine the validity and soundness of a deductive argument.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Recognize valid logical forms.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			A <term>logical argument</term><idx>argument</idx><idx><h>logical argument</h><see>argument</see></idx> is a claim that a set of propositions, called <term>premises</term>, supports a proposition called the <term>conclusion</term>.
			In this section we will look at inductive and deductive arguments and how to determine the strength, validity, and/or soundness of each. We will use truth tables as our primary tool for determining the validity of deductive arguments, but will also list several standard argument forms.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<subsection>
		<title>Inductive and Deductive Arguments</title>
		
		<p>
			The two types of arguments presented in this section are inductive and deductive arguments.  It is important to understand the difference between these two types because the analysis we apply is quite different. To better understand the difference between inductive and deductive arguments, let us begin by looking at a couple of examples.
		</p>
		<note>
			<p>
				To analyze an argument, it is helpful to precisely state its premises and conclusion. Most arguments you encounter in the real world won't be stated in a precise <q>premise, premise, conclusion</q> form. Sometimes the conclusion will be stated before the premises, or the premises will be hidden within a bunch of rhetoric.
			</p>
		</note>
		<example>
			<p>
				Consider the following argument:
			</p>
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						When I went to the store last week, I forgot my wallet, and I forgot it again when I went back today. I always forget my wallet when I go to the store.
					</p>
				</blockquote>
			<p>
				To begin our analysis of this first argument, let's first rewrite it in a more precise <q>premise, premise, conclusion</q> form.
			</p>
			<tabular>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
					<cell>I forgot my wallet when I went to the store last week.</cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
					<cell>I forgot my wallet when I went to the store today.</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
					<cell>I always forget my wallet when I go to the store.</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<p>
				Notice that both premises make a claim about a <alert>specific</alert> instance<mdash/>the specific instance last week when I forgot my wallet, and the specific instance today when I forgot my wallet. The conclusion, on the other hand, states what we can expect to happen more generally.
			</p>
		</example>
		<p>
			Now let's consider a different argument. As you read it, think about how it differs from the previous example.
		</p>
		<example>
			<p>
				Argument:
			</p>
			<blockquote>
				<p>
					Henry must know CPR because he is a nurse and all nurses know CPR.					
				</p>
			</blockquote>
			<p>
				Just as we did for the last example, let's rewrite the argument in its <q>premise, premise, conclusion</q> form for clarity:
			</p>
			<tabular>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
					<cell>All nurses know CPR.</cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
					<cell>Henry is a nurse.</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
					<cell>Henry knows CPR.</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<p>
				Unlike the first argument, where the premises were specific and the conclusion was general, this argument's first premise is a general statement and the conclusion is specific.
			</p>
		</example>
		<p>
			We can determine whether an argument is inductive or deductive by looking at which part of the argument is general and which part is specific. In the first example, the premises were specific and the conclusion was more general. This is an example of an inductive argument. In the second example, it was the premises that were more general and the conclusion that was specific. This is an example of a deductive argument.
		</p>
		<p>
			In general, an <term>inductive argument</term><idx><h>argument</h><h>inductive</h></idx><idx><h>inductive argument</h><see>argument, inductive</see></idx> uses a collection of <em>specific</em> examples (e.g., observations or data) as its premises and uses them to propose a <em>general</em> conclusion. This is shown in the image on the left below, where the argument moves from the narrow specific premises at the bottom up to the wide general conclusion at the top. A <term>deductive argument</term><idx><h>argument</h><h>deductive</h></idx><idx><h>deductive argument</h><see>argument, deductive</see></idx> uses a collection of <em>general statements</em> (e.g., definitions or rules) as its premises and uses them to propose a <em>specific conclusion</em>. This is shown in the image to the right below, where the argument moves from the wide general premises at the bottom to the narrow specific conclusion at the top.
		</p>
		<sidebyside widths="40% 40%">
			<image>
			<shortdescription>A pyramid upside down with the narrow end at the bottom for a specific premise, the middle section is another specific premise; the wide part at the top is for the general conclusion</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont, align=center,
							band/.style={draw=CustomB2, thick, top color=CustomB1!25, bottom color=CustomB1!55}]
						% inverted triangle: tip at (0,0), top edge at y=4.8, half-width = 2y/3
						\path[band] (0,0) -- (1.333,2) -- (-1.333,2) -- cycle;
						\path[band] (-1.333,2) -- (1.333,2) -- (2.267,3.4) -- (-2.267,3.4) -- cycle;
						\path[band] (-2.267,3.4) -- (2.267,3.4) -- (3.2,4.8) -- (-3.2,4.8) -- cycle;
						\node at (0,1.45) {Specific\\ Premise};
						\node at (0,2.7) {Specific Premise};
						\node at (0,4.1) {General Conclusion};
						\node at (0,-0.9) {\textbf{Inductive Argument}\\ (Specific to General)};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
			</image>
			<image>
			<shortdescription>A pyramid right-side up with the wide base at the bottom for a general premise; another general premise in the middle section, and a specific conclusion in the narrow part at the top</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont, align=center,
							band/.style={draw=CustomB2, thick, top color=CustomB1!25, bottom color=CustomB1!55}]
						% triangle: base at y=0, apex at (0,4.8), half-width = 2(4.8-y)/3
						\path[band] (-3.2,0) -- (3.2,0) -- (2.267,1.4) -- (-2.267,1.4) -- cycle;
						\path[band] (-2.267,1.4) -- (2.267,1.4) -- (1.333,2.8) -- (-1.333,2.8) -- cycle;
						\path[band] (-1.333,2.8) -- (1.333,2.8) -- (0,4.8) -- cycle;
						\node at (0,3.35) {Specific\\ Conclusion};
						\node at (0,2.1) {General Premise};
						\node at (0,0.7) {General Premise};
						\node at (0,-0.9) {\textbf{Deductive Argument}\\ (General to Specific)};
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</sidebyside>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Rewrite the following arguments in a precise <q>premise, premise, conclusion</q> form, and determine if the argument is inductive or deductive.
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							A whole number greater than one is prime if its only divisors are one and itself. Since 13 is greater than one and its only divisors are one and itself, 13 must be prime.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Juan's dog Goober is having puppies. All three of Goober's previous litters have had 5 puppies so Goober is bound to have 5 puppies in this litter as well. 
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<tabular>
							<row>
								<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
								<cell>If a whole number greater than one has only one and itself as divisors, the number is prime.</cell>
							</row>
							<row bottom="minor">
								<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
								<cell>The number 13 is greater than one, and its only divisors are one and itself.</cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
								<cell>13 is prime.</cell>
							</row>
						</tabular>
						<p>
							Since the premises are general definitions and properties of numbers and the conclusion is a specific statement about the number 13, the argument is <alert>deductive</alert>.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<tabular>
							<row>
								<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
								<cell>Goober is having puppies.</cell>
							</row>
							<row bottom="minor">
								<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
								<cell>Goober's last three litters had 5 puppies.</cell>
							</row>
							<row>
								<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
								<cell>Goober's current litter will have 5 puppies.</cell>
							</row>
						</tabular>
						<p>
							This is an example of an <alert>inductive argument</alert> since it uses specific instances (Goober's past litters) as its premises. The argument generalizes from these instances to a pattern (Goober always has 5 puppies) and applies that pattern to the current litter. Even if every past litter had 5 puppies, the conclusion is not guaranteed.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection>
		<title>Evaluating Arguments</title>
		<p>
			Inductive arguments cannot be proven. The best we can do is evaluate the <term>strength</term><idx><h>argument</h><h>strength of</h></idx> of the argument based on the evidence it provides.
		</p> 
		<p>
			A strong inductive argument is one that is well supported by its premises, while a weak inductive argument is one whose premises do a poor job of supporting the conclusion. The strength of an inductive argument is subjective, because where one person sees a strong argument, another may see a weak argument. Additionally, the strength and truth of an argument are not necessarily related; it is possible to have a weak argument whose conclusion is true, and a strong argument whose conclusion is false.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Determine the strength of the inductive argument. 
				</p>
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						Jamie Foxx, Jodie Foster, Jennifer Lawrence, and Jack Nicholson have all won Academy Awards for acting. Actors whose names start with J are bound to win an Academy Award.
					</p>
				</blockquote>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The inductive argument provides a number of specific cases as evidence for the conclusion. However, we would not be surprised if a J-named actor <em>did not</em> win an Academy Award, so the argument is weak.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			Deductive arguments, on the other hand, are evaluated by their validity and soundness. A deductive argument is <term>valid</term><idx><h>argument</h><h>valid</h></idx><idx><h>validity</h><see>argument, valid</see></idx> if its conclusion must be true whenever its premises are true; that is, the conclusion follows logically from the premises. A valid argument is <term>sound</term><idx><h>argument</h><h>sound</h></idx><idx><h>soundness</h><see>argument, sound</see></idx> if, in addition, its premises are actually true. An argument cannot be sound if it is not valid, even if the premises appear reasonable.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Determine whether the following argument is valid and whether it is sound.
				</p>
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						All birds can fly. Penguins are birds. Therefore, penguins can fly.
					</p>
				</blockquote>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<tabular>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
						<cell>All birds can fly.</cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
						<cell>Penguins are birds.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
						<cell>Penguins can fly.</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					The argument is <alert>valid</alert>: if all birds could fly and penguins are birds, then penguins would have to be able to fly. However, the first premise is false, since penguins (along with ostriches and emus) are birds that cannot fly. Because one of its premises is false, the argument is <alert>not sound</alert>, even though it is valid.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection>
		<title>Evaluating Deductive Arguments Using Truth Tables</title>
			<p>
				There are various ways to evaluate deductive logical arguments. Our focus here will be the use of truth tables. In this method, the verbal argument is translated into mathematical notation and all possible truth values of the statement are organized in a table. The argument is valid when the conclusion is true whenever all premises are true, and it is invalid when there is at least one scenario where the premises are true but the conclusion is false. 
				The advantage of this method is that it requires no knowledge of logical forms. However, it can be tedious for complicated problems, especially those requiring more than 2 or 3 propositions.
			</p>
			<example>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose each of the following statements is true:
						<ol>
							<li>Either the security system was activated or the alarm went off, but not both.</li>
							<li>The alarm did not go off.</li>
						</ol>
						Prove that <q>The security system was activated.</q>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						First, define the necessary logical variables:
						<ul>
							<li>Let <m>S</m> be <q>The security system was activated.</q></li>
							<li>Let <m>A</m> be <q>The alarm went off.</q></li>
						</ul>
						The first statement can be written as: <m>(S \lor A) \land \lnot(S \land A)</m>, which is more simply written using the symbol for the exclusive OR (see <xref ref="ex-logic-xor"/>), <m>S \oplus A</m>.
						Similarly, the second statement may be written as <m>\lnot A</m>, and the conclusion is simply <m>S</m>. 
						In its briefest form, the argument is thus
					</p>
					<tabular>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>S \oplus A</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>\lnot A</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>S</m>.</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						To evaluate this argument we need a truth table with columns for <m>S</m>, <m>A</m>, <m>S \oplus A</m>, and <m>\neg A</m>.
					</p>
					<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>C: <m>S</m></cell>
							<cell><m>A</m></cell>
							<cell>P1: <m>S \oplus A</m></cell>
							<cell>P2: <m>\neg A</m></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						To evaluate the argument, we ask if the conclusion is true every time the premises are true. Row two is the only row of the truth table in which <alert>P1</alert> and <alert>P2</alert> (columns 3 and 4) are both true, and the conclusion (first column) is true in that row as well. Thus, this is a <alert>valid</alert> argument.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</example>
			<p>
				Here is an example requiring three propositions.
			</p>
			<example>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose each of the following statements is true:
						<ol>
							<li>It is snowing.</li>
							<li>If it is warm, then it is not snowing.</li>
							<li>If it is not warm, then I cannot go swimming.</li>
						</ol>
						Prove: I cannot go swimming.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Begin by clearly defining the necessary logical variables:
						<ul>
							<li>Let <m>p</m> be <q>It is snowing.</q></li>
							<li>Let <m>q</m> be <q>It is warm.</q></li>
							<li>Let <m>r</m> be <q>I can go swimming.</q></li>
						</ul>
						You could write <m>r</m> as the negative <q>I cannot go swimming</q>, but writing the statements without negatives reduces the confusion of potential double negatives later in a problem. Using these symbols, the argument can be summarized as:
					</p>
					<tabular>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>p</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>q \rightarrow \neg p</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>\lnot q \rightarrow \neg r</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>\lnot r</m>.</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						Next, create a truth table for the above argument, where each premise and the conclusion appear in a column. Here is the table for this example.
					</p>
					<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>P1: <m>p</m></cell>
							<cell><m>q</m></cell>
							<cell><m>r</m></cell>
							<cell><m>\lnot p</m></cell>
							<cell><m>\lnot q</m></cell>
							<cell>C: <m>\lnot r</m></cell>
							<cell>P2: <m>q \to\lnot p</m></cell>
							<cell>P3: <m>\lnot q\to\lnot r</m></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						Row 4 is the only row in which all three premises are true, and the conclusion is true in that row as well. Therefore, the argument is valid.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</example>
			<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-logic-tabular-proof">
				<title>Summary: Tabular Method</title>
				<p>
					To test the validity of an argument (i.e., prove or disprove it):
					<ol>
						<li> Write the verbal argument in symbolic form.</li>
						<li> Draw a truth table where each premise and the conclusion gets its own column. Do this all in one (possibly large!) table.</li>
						<li>Locate all rows where <alert>all</alert> of the premises are true.</li>
						<li>Look at each row from the previous step. Is the conclusion also true? If it is also true on each row, then the argument is valid. Otherwise, the argument is not valid.</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</assemblage>
			<p>
				Even one instance where the premises are true and the conclusion is false invalidates an argument. It does not matter if there is another row in the table where the premises are true and the conclusion is true. The next example illustrates an invalid argument.
			</p>
			<example xml:id="ex-logic-invalid-argument-table">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Evaluate the validity of the following argument.
					</p>
					<blockquote>
						<p>
							If taxes are cut, then retail sales increase. If retail sales increase, then the economy improves. Thus, if taxes are not cut, the economy will not improve.
						</p>
					</blockquote>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						First, define the necessary logical variables:
						<ul>
							<li>Let <m>p</m> be <q>Taxes are cut.</q></li>
							<li>Let <m>q</m> be <q>Retail sales increase.</q></li>
							<li>Let <m>r</m> be <q>The economy improves.</q></li>
						</ul>
						The argument is summarized symbolically as
					</p>
					<tabular>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>p \to q</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row bottom="minor">
							<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>q \rightarrow r</m>,</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
							<cell><m>\lnot p \rightarrow \lnot r</m>.</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						Next, create a truth table for the above argument. Make sure each premise and the conclusion appear in a column. Here we number the rows for easy reference, but in general this is not necessary.
					</p>
					<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>row</cell>
							<cell><m>p</m></cell>
							<cell><m>q</m></cell>
							<cell><m>r</m></cell>
							<cell>P1: <m>p\to q</m></cell>
							<cell>P2: <m>q\to r</m></cell>
							<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
							<cell><m>\neg r</m></cell>
							<cell>C: <m>\neg p \to \neg r</m></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>1</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>2</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>3</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>4</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>5</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>6</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>7</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>8</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>F</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
							<cell>T</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						Notice that in rows 1 and 8 each premise and the conclusion are true. This might make you think the argument is valid. However, in rows 5 and 7 the premises are true but the conclusion is false! This demonstrates that the argument is <em>invalid</em>. To be valid <em>every</em> row where the premises are true must have a true conclusion as well.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</example>
			<p>
				<xref ref="ex-logic-invalid-argument-table"/> is invalid because it commits the fallacy of <term>denying the antecedent</term><idx><h>fallacy</h><h>denying the antecedent</h></idx><idx><h>denying the antecedent</h><see>fallacy, denying the antecedent</see></idx> (or <term>fallacy of the inverse</term><idx><h>fallacy of the inverse</h><see>fallacy, denying the antecedent</see></idx>), which is based on the mistaken assumption that a conditional and its inverse are equivalent, as described in <xref ref="fact-logic-equivalence-contrapositive"/>.
				Even with the premises being true, the economy could still improve for reasons completely unrelated to tax cuts and retail sales. If you are interested in learning more about common logical fallacies, consider reading the next section of this chapter, <xref ref="sec-logic-fallacies"/>.
			</p>
	</subsection>
	<subsection>
		<title>Forms of Valid Arguments (Optional)</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Although evaluating arguments by truth table is accurate and powerful, making the tables can be tedious and exhausting. Also, it is sometimes redundant, as arguments about different topics may have the same logical form. 
				If we are able to recognize certain common forms of arguments as valid (or invalid), we can evaluate validity without the tedium of creating a table. The following is a sampling of valid logical forms. Invalid logical forms (or fallacies) are discussed in <xref ref="sec-logic-fallacies"/>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<subsubsection>
			<title>The Law of Detachment</title>
			<p>
				Also called by its Latin name, <foreign>modus ponens</foreign><idx><h>modus ponens</h><see>law of detachment</see></idx> (<q>mode that affirms</q>), the law of detachment applies when a conditional and its antecedent are given as premises and the consequent is the conclusion. The general form is:
			</p>
			<tabular>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>p \to q</m>,</cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>p</m>,</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>q</m>.</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<fact>
				<title>The Law of Detachment</title>
				<idx>law of detachment</idx>
				<idx><h>logical rules</h><h>detachment</h></idx>		
				<statement>
					<p>
						If the conditional <m>p \to q</m> is true and <m>p</m> is true, then <m>q</m> must be true.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</fact>
			<example>
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						If you bought bread, then you went to the store. You bought bread. Therefore, you went to the store.
					</p>
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					This argument has the structure described by the law of detachment: the second premise and the conclusion are simply the two parts of the first premise detached from each other. Instead of making a truth table, we can say that this argument is valid by stating that it satisfies the law of detachment.
				</p>
			</example>
		</subsubsection>
		<subsubsection>
			<title>The Law of Contraposition</title>
			<p>
				The law of contraposition applies when a conditional and the negation of its consequent are given as premises, and the negation of its antecedent is the conclusion. This form is also called <foreign>modus tollens</foreign><idx><h>modus tollens</h><see>law of contraposition</see></idx> (<q>mode that denies</q>). The general form is:
			</p>
			<tabular>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>p \to q</m>,</cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>\lnot q</m>,</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>\lnot p</m>.</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<fact>
				<title>The Law of Contraposition</title>	
				<idx>law of contraposition</idx>
				<idx><h>logical rules</h><h>contraposition</h></idx>			
				<statement>
					<p>
						If the conditional <m>p \to q</m> is true and <m>\lnot q</m> is true, then <m>\lnot p</m> must be true.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</fact>
			<p>
				Notice that the second premise and the conclusion look like the contrapositive of the first premise, <m>\neg q \to \neg p</m>, but they have been detached. Think of the law of contraposition as a combination of the law of detachment and the fact that the contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement.
			</p>
			<example>
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						If I drop my phone into the swimming pool, my phone will be ruined. My phone isn't ruined. Therefore, I didn't drop my phone into the swimming pool.
					</p>
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					The form of this argument matches what we need to invoke the law of contraposition, so it is a valid argument.
				</p>
			</example>
		</subsubsection>
		<subsubsection>
			<title>The Transitive Property</title>
			<p>
				The transitive property (or <term>hypothetical syllogism</term><idx><h>hypothetical syllogism</h><see>transitive property</see></idx>) has as its premises a series of conditionals, where the consequent of one is the antecedent of the next. The conclusion is a conditional with the same antecedent as the first premise and the same consequent as the final premise. 
				In other words, the transitive property describes a chain reaction: if the first thing happens, then the second thing happens, and if the second thing happens, then the third thing happens. Therefore, skipping over the middle step, we can say that if the first thing happens, then we know the third thing will happen. 
				The general form is:
			</p>
			<tabular>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>p \to q</m>,</cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>q\to r</m>,</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
					<cell><m>p \to r</m>.</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
			<fact>
				<title>The Transitive Property</title>		
				<idx>transitive property</idx>
				<idx><h>logical rules</h><h>transitivity</h></idx>		
				<statement>
					<p>
						If the conditional <m>p \to q</m> is true and the conditional <m>q \to r</m> is true, then <m>p \to r</m> must be true.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</fact>
			<example>
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						If Hayley commits a reckless foul, she will receive a yellow card. If Hayley receives a yellow card, she will be suspended for the next match. Therefore, if Hayley commits a reckless foul, she will be suspended for the next match.
					</p>
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					This argument has the exact structure required to use the transitive property, so it is a valid argument.
				</p>
			</example>
		</subsubsection>
	</subsection>

	<exercises>
		<!-- Sources: Exercise 2 is adapted from Benjamin Jackson's MATH 105
		Written Homework question 07a, Exercise 5a-b from question 08a, and
		Exercise 9 from question 08c (Walla Walla University, Fall 2026).
		Exercises 12a-b and 13 are adapted from his MATH 105 Objective 8
		slides, Exercises 10 and 11 from his Objective 8 practice handout, and
		Exercise 14 from his Objective 8 activity. Exercises 1, 3, 4, 5c, 6, 7,
		8, and 12c-e are new.
		-->
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise><!-- #1 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Determine whether each argument is inductive or deductive.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Anyone who scores 90% or higher on the final exam earns an A on it. Theo scored 93% on the final exam. Therefore, Theo earned an A on the final exam.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Every apple I have picked from this tree has been sour. Therefore, all the apples on this tree are sour.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Ana has been late to every Monday meeting this month. Therefore, she will be late to next Monday's meeting.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Every planet in our solar system orbits the Sun. Mars is a planet in our solar system. Therefore, Mars orbits the Sun.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Deductive. It reasons from a general rule about everyone who scores 90% or higher to a specific conclusion about Theo.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Inductive. It reasons from the specific apples I have picked to a general conclusion about every apple on the tree.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Inductive. It reasons from specific past meetings to a pattern (Ana is late on Mondays) and applies that pattern to the next meeting. The conclusion is likely, but not guaranteed.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Deductive. It reasons from a general statement about the planets to a specific conclusion about Mars.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #2 (Written Homework question 07a) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Rewrite each argument in <q>premise, premise, conclusion</q> form, and determine whether it is inductive or deductive. If the argument is inductive, describe its strength. If it is deductive, decide whether it is valid and whether it is sound.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								All bicycles have two wheels. My friend's Harley-Davidson has two wheels, so it must be a bicycle.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Kiran asked their friends what time they wake up on weekends. Every friend said they sleep past 9 a.m., so Kiran concluded that all college students sleep past 9 a.m. on weekends.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Since all cats are scared of vacuum cleaners and Max is a cat, Max must be scared of vacuum cleaners.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell>All bicycles have two wheels.</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell>My friend's Harley-Davidson has two wheels.</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell>My friend's Harley-Davidson is a bicycle.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								This argument is deductive, since it reasons from a general statement about bicycles to a specific vehicle. It is <alert>invalid</alert>, and therefore not sound: P1 says that if something is a bicycle, then it has two wheels, but that does not mean that everything with two wheels is a bicycle. (This mistake is called <em>affirming the consequent</em>.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell>Every one of Kiran's friends sleeps past 9 a.m. on weekends.</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell>All college students sleep past 9 a.m. on weekends.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								This argument is inductive, since it reasons from specific people to a general conclusion. It is <alert>weak</alert>: Kiran's friends are a small group, and probably not a representative sample of all college students.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell>All cats are scared of vacuum cleaners.</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell>Max is a cat.</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell>Max is scared of vacuum cleaners.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								This argument is deductive and <alert>valid</alert>: if both premises were true, the conclusion would have to be true. However, it is <alert>not sound</alert>, because P1 is false; some cats are not scared of vacuum cleaners.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #3 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Each of the following arguments is inductive. Decide whether each is strong or weak, and explain why.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								A survey of 2,000 randomly selected adults in Washington found that 58% of them own a pet. Therefore, about 58% of adults in Washington own a pet.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								My two roommates both love pineapple on pizza. Therefore, most people love pineapple on pizza.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The 7:40 bus has arrived late every weekday for the past three months. Therefore, it will arrive late tomorrow.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Strong. The sample is large and randomly chosen from the group the conclusion is about, so it is likely to be representative.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Weak. Two roommates are far too few people to support a conclusion about most people, and roommates may share tastes.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Fairly strong. The pattern has held for many days in a row, so we would expect it to continue, but something could change (a new driver or a new schedule), so the conclusion is not guaranteed.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #4 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Fill in the blank to complete each argument. Then state whether the argument is inductive or deductive.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								All birds are <fillin characters="10"/>. A robin is a bird. Therefore, a robin is red.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								For each of the past ten years, the first snow in our town fell in November. Therefore, this year <fillin characters="30"/>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Every triangle has three sides. This shape is a triangle. Therefore, <fillin characters="25"/>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								All birds are <em>red</em>. (Deductive; it is valid, but not sound, since not all birds are red.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								This year <em>the first snow will fall in November</em>. (Inductive)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Therefore, <em>this shape has three sides</em>. (Deductive)
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #5 (parts a and b from Written Homework question 08a; part c new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Decide whether each argument is valid. If it is valid, decide whether it is sound. Explain your answers.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								All animals have four legs. A giraffe has four legs. Therefore, a giraffe is an animal.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								All mathematicians are nice people. Your professor is a mathematician. Therefore, your professor is a nice person.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Everyone who is registered to vote in the United States is at least 18 years old. Jordan is 19 years old. Therefore, Jordan is registered to vote.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<alert>Invalid</alert>, and therefore not sound. Let <m>a</m> be <q>It is an animal</q> and <m>f</m> be <q>It has four legs.</q> The argument has the form <m>a \to f</m>, <m>f</m>, therefore <m>a</m>. In the row of the truth table where <m>a</m> is false and <m>f</m> is true, both premises are true but the conclusion is false. In words, having four legs does not make something an animal (a table has four legs, too). The conclusion happens to be true, but it does not follow from the premises. (The first premise is also false: birds are animals.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<alert>Valid</alert>. Let <m>m</m> be <q>It is a mathematician</q> and <m>n</m> be <q>It is a nice person.</q> The argument has the form <m>m \to n</m>, <m>m</m>, therefore <m>n</m>, which is the law of detachment. Whether it is sound depends on whether <em>every</em> mathematician is a nice person, which is hard to believe, so the argument is probably <alert>not sound</alert>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<alert>Invalid</alert>, and therefore not sound. P1 says that if someone is registered to vote, then they are at least 18; it does not say that everyone who is at least 18 is registered. Jordan may never have registered. This is the same mistake as in part a.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #6 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write an argument whose premises and conclusion are all true, but which is <em>not</em> valid.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Write an argument that is valid but has a false conclusion.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Explain why a valid argument with a false conclusion must have at least one false premise.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Answers to parts a and b will vary. Sample answers:
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<q>If a number is divisible by 4, then it is even. The number 8 is even. Therefore, 8 is divisible by 4.</q> Each statement is true, but the conclusion does not follow from the premises: the number 6 is also even, and it is not divisible by 4.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<q>All planets have moons. Mercury is a planet. Therefore, Mercury has a moon.</q> The conclusion would follow if the premises were true, so the argument is valid, but Mercury has no moons. (The first premise is false.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								In a valid argument, the conclusion must be true whenever all of the premises are true. So if the conclusion is false, the premises cannot all be true; at least one of them is false. This means a valid argument with a false conclusion is never sound.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #7 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Each truth table below belongs to an argument with premises labeled P and conclusion C. The columns marked <m>*</m> are intermediate steps. Decide whether each argument is valid, and explain how you can tell.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>*</m></cell>
									<cell>P1</cell>
									<cell><m>*</m></cell>
									<cell>P2</cell>
									<cell>P3</cell>
									<cell>C</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell>P1</cell>
									<cell><m>*</m></cell>
									<cell>P2</cell>
									<cell>C</cell>
									<cell><m>*</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<alert>Valid</alert>. All three premises are true only in row 3, and the conclusion is true in that row.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<alert>Invalid</alert>. Both premises are true in rows 1 and 4, but the conclusion is false in row 4.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #8 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use a truth table to decide whether each argument is valid.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to q</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>q \to p</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor q</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \land q</m>.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor q</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to \neg q</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg q</m>.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell>P1: <m>p \to q</m></cell>
									<cell>P2: <m>q \to p</m></cell>
									<cell>P3: <m>p \lor q</m></cell>
									<cell>C: <m>p \land q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								All three premises are true only in row 1, and the conclusion is true in that row, so the argument is <alert>valid</alert>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell>P1: <m>p \lor q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
									<cell>P2: <m>p \to \neg q</m></cell>
									<cell>C: <m>\neg q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								Both premises are true in rows 2 and 3. The conclusion is false in row 3, so the argument is <alert>invalid</alert>.
