						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #9 (Written Homework question 08c; Obj 8 slides) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose each of the following statements is true:
				</p>
				<tabular>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
						<cell>If I enjoy studying, then I will study.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
						<cell>I will do my homework or I will not study.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
						<cell>I will not do my homework.</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Use a truth table to show that <q>I do not enjoy studying</q> must be true.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>p</m> be <q>I enjoy studying,</q> <m>q</m> be <q>I will study,</q> and <m>r</m> be <q>I will do my homework.</q> The premises are <m>p \to q</m>, <m>r \lor \neg q</m>, and <m>\neg r</m>, and the conclusion is <m>\neg p</m>.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>row</cell>
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>r</m></cell>
						<cell>P1: <m>p \to q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
						<cell>P2: <m>r \lor \neg q</m></cell>
						<cell>P3: <m>\neg r</m></cell>
						<cell>C: <m>\neg p</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>1</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>2</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>3</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>4</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>5</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>6</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>7</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>8</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Row 8 is the only row in which all three premises are true, and the conclusion is true in that row as well, so the argument is valid. Therefore, <q>I do not enjoy studying</q> must be true.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #10 (Obj 8 practice handout) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					An art student is deciding whether to pursue a creative career. She reasons, <q>Either I pursue financial security or I pursue my passion for art. If I pursue financial security, I will be content. If I pursue my passion for art, I will be content. Therefore, I will be content.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write the argument in symbolic form. (Hint: you need three propositions.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use a truth table to decide whether the argument is valid.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>p</m> be <q>I pursue financial security,</q> <m>q</m> be <q>I pursue my passion for art,</q> and <m>r</m> be <q>I will be content.</q>
							</p>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor q</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to r</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>q \to r</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>r</m>.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>r</m></cell>
									<cell>P1: <m>p \lor q</m></cell>
									<cell>P2: <m>p \to r</m></cell>
									<cell>P3: <m>q \to r</m></cell>
									<cell>C: <m>r</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>5</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>6</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>7</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>8</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								All three premises are true in rows 1, 3, and 5, and the conclusion is true in each of them, so the argument is <alert>valid</alert>. (Whether it is sound depends on whether both choices really would make her content.)
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #11 (Obj 8 practice handout) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Someone makes the following argument: <q>If people exercise regularly, then they maintain a healthy weight. If people maintain a healthy weight, then they have low cholesterol. Mel does not exercise regularly. Therefore, Mel does not have low cholesterol.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write the argument in symbolic form.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use a truth table to decide whether the argument is valid.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>p</m> be <q>Mel exercises regularly,</q> <m>q</m> be <q>Mel maintains a healthy weight,</q> and <m>r</m> be <q>Mel has low cholesterol.</q>
							</p>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to q</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>q \to r</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg p</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg r</m>.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>r</m></cell>
									<cell>P1: <m>p \to q</m></cell>
									<cell>P2: <m>q \to r</m></cell>
									<cell>P3: <m>\neg p</m></cell>
									<cell>C: <m>\neg r</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>5</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>6</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>7</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>8</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								All three premises are true in rows 5, 7, and 8, but the conclusion is false in rows 5 and 7, so the argument is <alert>invalid</alert>. In words, Mel could have low cholesterol for some other reason. This argument commits the fallacy of denying the antecedent.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #12 (parts a and b from the Obj 8 slides; parts c to e new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					For each argument, decide whether it is valid. If it is valid, name the form it uses (law of detachment, law of contraposition, or transitive property). If it is not valid, explain why.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								If today is Tuesday, then the library closes early. Today is Tuesday. Therefore, the library closes early.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If I don't wake up, then I can't go to work. If I can't go to work, then I won't get paid. Therefore, if I don't wake up, then I won't get paid.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If the package had been delivered, then the doorbell camera would have recorded it. The doorbell camera recorded nothing. Therefore, the package was not delivered.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If you studied, then you passed. You passed. Therefore, you studied.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If you studied, then you passed. You did not study. Therefore, you did not pass.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Valid, by the law of detachment.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Valid, by the transitive property.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Valid, by the law of contraposition.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Not valid. The argument has the form <m>p \to q</m>, <m>q</m>, therefore <m>p</m>. You could have passed without studying. (This fallacy is called <em>affirming the consequent</em>.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Not valid. The argument has the form <m>p \to q</m>, <m>\neg p</m>, therefore <m>\neg q</m>, which is the fallacy of denying the antecedent. Again, you could have passed without studying.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #13 (Obj 8 slides) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose each of the following statements is true:
				</p>
				<tabular>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
						<cell>If Dan is happy, then Bill is happy.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
						<cell>If Sandy is not happy, then Bill is not happy.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
						<cell>If Dan is not happy, then Bill is not happy.</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Is <q>If Dan is happy, then Sandy is happy</q> a valid conclusion? Answer using a truth table or using the valid argument forms.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>p</m> be <q>Dan is happy,</q> <m>q</m> be <q>Bill is happy,</q> and <m>r</m> be <q>Sandy is happy.</q> The premises are <m>p \to q</m>, <m>\neg r \to \neg q</m>, and <m>\neg p \to \neg q</m>, and the conclusion is <m>p \to r</m>.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>row</cell>
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>r</m></cell>
						<cell>P1: <m>p \to q</m></cell>
						<cell>P2: <m>\neg r \to \neg q</m></cell>
						<cell>P3: <m>\neg p \to \neg q</m></cell>
						<cell>C: <m>p \to r</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>1</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>2</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>3</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>4</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>5</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>6</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>7</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>8</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					All three premises are true in rows 1, 7, and 8, and the conclusion is true in each of them, so the conclusion is <alert>valid</alert>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Using the argument forms instead: P2 is the contrapositive of <m>q \to r</m>, so it is equivalent to <q>If Bill is happy, then Sandy is happy.</q> Combining this with P1 by the transitive property gives <m>p \to r</m>. (P3 is not needed.)
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #14 (Obj 8 activity) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					The following is a version of a classic argument, often attributed to the Greek philosopher Epicurus (about 300 BCE), that tries to disprove the existence of an all-powerful, all-good God.
				</p>
				<tabular>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
						<cell>If God exists, then God is good enough to want to prevent evil and powerful enough to get rid of evil.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
						<cell>If God is good enough to want to prevent evil and powerful enough to get rid of evil, then evil does not exist.</cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
						<cell>Evil exists.</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
						<cell>Therefore, God does not exist.</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The argument contains four propositions. Name them <m>p</m>, <m>q</m>, <m>r</m>, and <m>s</m>, and write the argument in symbolic form.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use the transitive property to combine P1 and P2 into a single premise. Your new argument should have only two premises and two propositions.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use a truth table to decide whether the argument is valid.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Is the argument sound? If you wanted to argue against its conclusion, which premise would you challenge, and why?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>p</m> be <q>God exists,</q> <m>q</m> be <q>God is good enough to want to prevent evil,</q> <m>r</m> be <q>God is powerful enough to get rid of evil,</q> and <m>s</m> be <q>Evil exists.</q>
							</p>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P1:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to (q \land r)</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>P2:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>(q \land r) \to \neg s</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>P3:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>s</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg p</m>.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>Q1:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to \neg s</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row bottom="minor">
									<cell><alert>Q2:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>s</m>,</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell><alert>C:</alert></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg p</m>.</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>s</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg s</m></cell>
									<cell>Q1: <m>p \to \neg s</m></cell>
									<cell>Q2: <m>s</m></cell>
									<cell>C: <m>\neg p</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								Both premises are true only in row 3, and the conclusion is true there, so the argument is <alert>valid</alert>. (It is an example of the law of contraposition.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Answers will vary. Because the argument is valid, the only way to reject its conclusion is to reject one of its premises. Many people challenge P2: for example, a good and powerful God might allow evil for the sake of a greater good, such as human free will. Others question what <q>all-powerful</q> or <q>good</q> means in P1. The point is that whether the argument is sound depends on whether its premises are true, which logic alone cannot settle.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

	</exercises>
</section>